ADS-008 — Canonical Interface Contract (CIC)

Document ID: ADS-008
Title: Canonical Interface Contract (CIC)
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Interface Contract (CIC), the standardized representation of interaction agreements within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The CIC specifies the semantics, capabilities, constraints, inputs, outputs, quality expectations, and governance required for interoperable communication between Atlas participants.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Interface model
Contract structure
Capability model
Input and output specifications
Constraints
Versioning
Governance
Conformance

This specification applies to every interface exposed within the Atlas ecosystem.

3. Canonical Definition

A Canonical Interface Contract (CIC) is a specialization of the Canonical Atlas Object that formally defines the semantic, functional, operational, and governance requirements governing interactions between Atlas participants.

The CIC specifies what an interaction means, not how it is implemented.

4. Objectives

The CIC SHALL:

Standardize interfaces.
Preserve semantic consistency.
Enable interoperability.
Support independent evolution.
Enable validation.
Support governance.
Ensure discoverability.
Support version compatibility.
5. Inheritance

The Canonical Interface Contract SHALL inherit all mandatory properties defined by the Canonical Atlas Object.

Canonical Atlas Object
        │
        ▼
Interface Contract
        │
 ┌──────┼──────────────┐
 ▼      ▼              ▼
Service Agent      External
Contract Contract  Contract

Future interface profiles SHALL extend this hierarchy.

6. Interface Categories

Atlas defines the following canonical interface categories.

Category	Purpose
Cognitive Interface	Exchanges engineering cognition
Governance Interface	Supports governance operations
Workflow Interface	Coordinates workflows
Platform Interface	Provides platform services
Integration Interface	Connects external systems
Administrative Interface	Supports administration
Human Interface	Supports human interaction
7. Mandatory Contract Properties

Every CIC SHALL define:

Property	Description
Contract ID	Globally unique identifier
Contract Name	Human-readable name
Interface Category	Canonical classification
Provider	Service exposing the interface
Consumer	Intended participant(s)
Purpose	Functional objective
Version	Contract version
Status	Lifecycle status
8. Capability Model

Each interface SHALL declare one or more capabilities.

Each capability SHALL specify:

Capability identifier
Capability description
Preconditions
Postconditions
Supported operations
Constraints
Expected outcomes

Capabilities SHALL be independently identifiable.

9. Input and Output Model

Each capability SHALL define:

Inputs
Accepted object types
Required properties
Optional properties
Validation rules
Outputs
Produced object types
Expected outcomes
Error conditions
Completion semantics

Inputs and outputs SHALL reference Canonical Atlas Objects.

10. Constraint Model

A CIC SHALL explicitly define:

Functional constraints
Business constraints
Engineering constraints
Security constraints
Performance constraints
Regulatory constraints

Constraints SHALL be machine-readable where practical.

11. Versioning Model

Every CIC SHALL define:

Major version
Minor version
Patch version

Compatibility SHALL be classified as:

Compatibility	Meaning
Backward Compatible	Existing consumers continue to operate
Forward Compatible	New providers support older consumers
Adapter Compatible	Compatibility achieved through adapters
Breaking Change	Requires consumer modification
12. Lifecycle

Every CIC SHALL support the following lifecycle.

Proposed
    │
Reviewed
    │
Approved
    │
Published
    │
Operational
    │
Deprecated
    │
Retired

Lifecycle transitions SHALL be governed and auditable.

13. Governance

Every CIC SHALL define:

Owner
Steward
Approval authority
Validation policy
Security policy
Deprecation policy
Change management policy

Governance SHALL remain attached throughout the contract lifecycle.

14. Quality Requirements

Every CIC SHALL satisfy:

Correctness
Completeness
Consistency
Stability
Interoperability
Discoverability
Traceability
Testability

Quality SHALL be measurable.

15. Conformance

A Canonical Interface Contract conforms if it:

Inherits the CAO contract.
Defines mandatory interface properties.
Declares capabilities.
Specifies inputs and outputs.
Defines constraints.
Supports lifecycle management.
Implements semantic versioning.
16. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Dynamic capability negotiation.
Semantic interface discovery.
AI-assisted contract generation.
Federated interface registries.
Self-describing interfaces.
Domain-specific interface profiles.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical interface semantics.

